the protein data bank archive is an  online repository of 3d structures of  proteins and nucleic acids all  structures in the pdb were  experimentally determined and for every  structure the pdb contains the x y and z  coordinates of each atom  rcspdb is one of the organizations that  manages the pdb archive it makes the  data publicly available on rcsb.org  where it also provides tools for query  analysis and visualization  rcsb pdb also maintains an educational  portal pdb101 accessible to everyone  free of charge at pdb101.rcsb.org  pdb101 offers a diverse array of  teaching resources highlighting the  fundamental connection between the 3d  structure of a protein and its  biological function  the materials are organized by type  accessible through the top menu and by  topic accessible through the browser  one of the key resources on pdb 101 is  molecule of the month  each column focuses on one structure  from the pdb archive and includes an  introduction to the structure and  function of the molecule a discussion of  the molecules relevance to human health  and welfare and an interactive viewer  enabling exploration of relevant  molecular features in 3d  under the learn tab pdb 101 features  various resources that can supplement  any curriculum with 3d structure driven  activities for all types of learners  looking for hands-on learning resources  bring pdb 101's hands-on paper models  into the classroom building these models  will help students to become familiar  with key aspects of protein structure  additionally pdb 101 provides 3d  printable models that highlight  interactions between a protein and its  ligands  pdb 101 also features molecular  animations explaining general topics  such as what proteins are and how  enzymes function as well as more focused  topics like pain signaling and ion pumps  pdb101 also contains interactive  animations that enable students to  digitally explore the world of proteins  for example the interactive molecular  machinery poster provides a proteomic  tour from the cell membrane to the  nucleus offering 3d views for each of  the proteins featured  looking for a class project where  students can combine research and  science communication the video  challenge for high school students is an  annual event where students research a  medically relevant science topic and  create a two-minute video combining the  topic's molecular and public health  aspects  pdb 101 also offers a variety of  comprehensive curricular modules under  the teach tab covering topics ranging  from biomolecular structures and models  to molecular immunology  these modules are fully outfitted with  learning objectives hands-on activities  and before and after tests that enable  teachers to assess student understanding  finally if you would like to bring  advanced 3d protein structure  visualization into the classroom the  interactive molestar viewer is available  on rcsb.org  the mall star viewer is accessible from  the structure summary page of a given  protein entry in the pdb archive  a user guide and video tutorial are also  available for the mall star viewer  please visit rcsb.org and pdb101.rcsp  today to begin your journey into the  world of proteins  you
 

